WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:03.980
If you're not here, please raise your hand.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.980
In Montgomery County, Maryland, long

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.889
one of the nation's most admired school
systems, huge numbers of high schoolers

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:16.049
are graduating despite repeatedly
skipping required classes. In 2018, out of

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:22.949
11,000 graduates, 1,800 had 20 or more
unexcused absences — in one class! During

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:27.449
the single 90-day semester! Schools need
to rethink how they're approaching

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:33.149
required seat time, but that can't mean
simply letting students skip school. Some

00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:36.540
students do have legitimate trouble
getting to campus, but the same

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.719
administrators who tell teachers they
won't tolerate failure

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:44.610
can't excuse rampant absenteeism and
then expect teachers to teach students

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.730
who aren't there! There are many
incentives to get high schoolers that

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.320
diploma. District and state officials
want rising graduation rates, families

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.510
want kids to graduate, and teachers don't
want to be seen as troublemakers. So be

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.680
wary: If a close look revealed this
craziness in one of the nation's most

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.660
respected school districts, there's a
good chance it's an issue in your local schools too.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.500
Should high schools have an
attendance requirement for graduation?

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.100
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know
what other topics you'd like our

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.360
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be
sure to like and subscribe for more

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.369
research and videos from AEI.

